---
// Written against SITE.datafastScriptUrl, which is rendered verbatim below. If
// the script ever changes, this page changes in the same commit — that is the
// rule in docs/phase0-todos.md item D, and rendering the real URL is what keeps
// it enforceable instead of aspirational.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site.js';

const title = `Privacy — what this site collects · ${SITE.name}`;
const description =
  'One third-party script, no cookies, no accounts, no ad tech. What DataFast records, what stays in your browser, and why there is no cookie banner.';
---

<Base {title} {description}>
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p>
      The short version: this site loads one third-party script, sets no cookies, and has no
      accounts to attach anything to. There is no banner because there is nothing to ask you about.
    </p>

    <h2>What this site loads</h2>
    <p>Exactly one request leaves your browser for a domain that isn't this one:</p>
    <p><code class="url">{SITE.datafastScriptUrl}</code></p>
    <p>
      That is <a href="https://datafa.st" rel="noopener">DataFast</a>, the analytics that tell us
      which pages people actually read. Everything else is served from this domain — the fonts are
      bundled at build time rather than fetched from Google, and the only other script is the few
      lines that run the theme toggle. No tag manager, no ad network, no embedded video, no comment
      widget, no session recorder, no affiliate tracker.
    </p>

    <h2>What DataFast collects</h2>
    <p>
      That URL is DataFast's <strong>cookieless</strong> build, not their default one. Rather than
      describe it in the abstract, here is every field the script puts in the request, read off the
      script itself:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>href</code>, <code>referrer</code>, <code>domain</code> — the page you're on and
        whatever linked you to it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>language</code>, <code>timezone</code>, <code>screenWidth</code>,
        <code>screenHeight</code>, <code>viewport</code> — your browser's language, IANA time zone,
        and window dimensions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>adClickIds</code> — ad-click parameters if the link you followed carried any
        (<code>gclid</code>, <code>fbclid</code>, <code>msclkid</code> and five others). This site
        buys no ads, so in practice it is empty.
      </li>
      <li><code>visitorId</code>, <code>sessionId</code> — the pseudonymous ids described below.</li>
      <li><code>cookieless: true</code> — the script telling the server which build it is.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Your IP address and user-agent aren't in that list because every HTTP request carries them
      regardless. DataFast's documentation says both are used as inputs to the visitor hash, and
      country-level location is derived from the address at their end rather than asked of your
      browser. There is no geolocation prompt here and never will be.
    </p>
    <p>
      To count you as one visitor rather than six, it derives a pseudonymous identifier on their
      server: a hash of signals including your IP address, your browser's user-agent, this site's
      domain, and a salt that rotates roughly every 24 hours (UTC). When that salt rotates,
      yesterday's identifier can no longer be matched to today's. This is a trade we made
      knowingly — it makes returning-visitor numbers fuzzier, and it means nothing durable follows
      you.
    </p>

    <h2>What is kept in your browser</h2>
    <p>Two things, both first-party, both strictly functional:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Three <code>sessionStorage</code> keys from DataFast —
        <code>datafast_visitor_id</code>, <code>datafast_session_id</code> and
        <code>datafast_pageview_state</code> — so that one visit isn't counted as several. Session
        storage dies with the tab; close it and they are gone.
      </li>
      <li>
        The light or dark theme you picked, if you touched the toggle. That is the only
        <code>localStorage</code> key this site writes. It stays until you clear it and never
        leaves your device.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      No cookies, from us or from DataFast. We read the script rather than taking a vendor's word
      for it: the cookieless build contains no reference to <code>document.cookie</code> anywhere,
      and writes to <code>localStorage</code> exactly never. Their default build, which we do not
      use, references it twice.
    </p>

    <h2>Why there is no cookie banner</h2>
    <p>
      Banners exist to ask permission to store or read a tracking identifier on your device. This
      site stores no such identifier, so there is no permission to ask for. A banner here would be
      theatre — and the tell for theatre is that clicking either button changes nothing about what
      actually happens.
    </p>

    <h2>Opting out</h2>
    <p>
      Before it sends anything, DataFast's script checks one flag. Set it from your browser's
      console on this site and the script stays quiet permanently — no account, no request to us,
      nothing for us to honour or quietly ignore:
    </p>
    <pre><code>localStorage.setItem('datafast_ignore', 'true')</code></pre>
    <p>
      Blocking <code>datafa.st</code> in a content blocker works just as well. Every page here is
      plain HTML that renders without any of this: nothing on this site is gated behind analytics
      loading, and nothing breaks when it doesn't.
    </p>

    <h2>What we can't tell you</h2>
    <p>
      DataFast is someone else's service, and this page won't claim more certainty than we have.
      Their documentation describes the cookieless identifier as derived from your IP address; it
      does not publish whether the raw address is discarded after hashing, or which jurisdiction
      the data is processed in. We asked the documentation, not a salesperson, and that is where it
      stops. If the answer matters to you, their
      <a href="https://datafa.st/gdpr" rel="noopener">GDPR page</a> is the primary source and you
      should read it rather than our paraphrase.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site is static and served by Cloudflare. Like every web host, Cloudflare sees the
      requests that reach it — address, user-agent, and URL — and handles them under its own terms.
      Self-hosting this site is on the roadmap, for the same reason you're reading a directory
      about self-hosting.
    </p>

    <h2>What this site never does</h2>
    <p>
      No accounts, no logins, no newsletter, no comment box, no ads, no affiliate links, and no
      selling or sharing of anything with anyone. There is nothing here to sell, which is the only
      privacy promise that has ever been worth much.
    </p>

    <h2>When this page changes</h2>
    <p>
      Before the site does, not after. If the tracking script is ever swapped for one that sets
      cookies, this page is wrong the moment it happens — so the rule is that the copy changes in
      the same commit as the script, or the script doesn't change. The URL above is rendered
      straight from the site's configuration, so it cannot quietly drift out of date.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: 68ch;
    padding-block: 3rem 4rem;
  }
  .prose h1 {
    margin-bottom: 0.5em;
  }
  .prose h2 {
    margin-top: 2em;
  }
  .prose ul {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }
  .url {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
</style>
